  "body": {
    "id": "thm:test-optimization-not-sharp",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "Let \\(t=\\min\\{\\gamma,\\alpha+\\beta\\}\\) and define\n\\[\n b_n=\\begin{cases}\n n^{-2t/(4t+d)},&d\\le4t,\\\\\n n^{-4t/(4t+3d)},&d>4t.\n \\end{cases}\n\\]\nFor every fixed \\((\\alpha,\\beta,\\gamma,d,L)\\), there are constants \\(0<c<C<\\infty\\) and \\(n_0\\) such that\n\\[\n c n^{-2\\gamma/(4\\gamma+d)}\\le\\rho_n^*\\le Cb_n\n\\]\nfor every \\(n\\ge n_0\\). This is an honest all-regime bracket, not a matched frontier. Moreover, for \\(d=\\gamma=1\\), \\(\\alpha=1/10\\), \\(\\beta=9/10\\), and every fixed \\(L\\ge2\\), the frozen testing optimization satisfies \\(r_{\\mathrm{test}}(n)\\ge4\\{\\Lambda_n/n\\}^{1/4}\\) for all sufficiently large \\(n\\), while \\(\\rho_n^*\\asymp n^{-2/5}\\). Hence \\(r_{\\mathrm{test}}(n)/\\rho_n^*\\to\\infty\\), and the common sharp rate requested in the source question cannot be defined from \\(r_{\\mathrm{test}}\\) and \\(r_{\\mathrm{mix}}\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "d",
      "gamma",
      "alpha",
      "beta",
      "L",
      "rTest",
      "n",
      "lambdaCal",
      "rhoStar",
      "rMix"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:test-envelope",
      "def:test-optimization",
      "def:mixture-optimization",
      "def:observational-critical-radius",
      "def:known-propensity-tests",
      "def:known-propensity-radius",
      "lem:known-propensity-benchmark",
      "prop:single-scale-upper"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "Every observational test becomes a known-propensity test by ignoring the supplied propensity, so the definitions of the two experiments imply \\(\\rho_{n,e}^*\\le\\rho_n^*\\). The lower bound in \\(\\texttt{lem:known-propensity-benchmark}\\) therefore gives\n\\[\n \\rho_n^*\\ge c n^{-2\\gamma/(4\\gamma+d)}.                              \\tag{38}\n\\]\nThe test and deterministic tuning in \\(\\texttt{prop:single-scale-upper}\\) give \\(\\rho_n^*\\le Cb_n\\), with \\(b_n\\) exactly as displayed in the statement. This proves the all-regime bracket.\n\nFor every \\(\\mathbf k\\in\\mathcal K_n\\), the \\(q=1\\) stochastic term in the frozen envelope is independent of the rank and equals\n\\[\n S_1(\\mathbf k)\n =\\left(\\frac{2^8\\Lambda_n}{n}\\right)^{1/2}\n =16\\left(\\frac{\\Lambda_n}{n}\\right)^{1/2}.                          \\tag{39}\n\\]\nAll bias and stochastic summands are nonnegative. Therefore\n\\[\n U_n(\\mathbf k)\n \\ge\\min\\left\\{1,\\sqrt{S_1(\\mathbf k)}\\right\\}\n =\\min\\left\\{1,4\\left(\\frac{\\Lambda_n}{n}\\right)^{1/4}\\right\\}.       \\tag{40}\n\\]\nBecause \\(\\Lambda_n=O(\\log n)\\), the second argument of the minimum is below one for all sufficiently large \\(n\\). Taking the minimum over \\(\\mathcal K_n\\) in (40) proves\n\\[\n r_{\\mathrm{test}}(n)\\ge4\\left(\\frac{\\Lambda_n}{n}\\right)^{1/4}.     \\tag{41}\n\\]\nFor the displayed parameter values, \\(\\alpha+\\beta=\\gamma=1\\) and \\(d=1\\le4\\gamma\\). Both sides of the all-regime bracket are then \\(n^{-2/5}\\), so \\(\\rho_n^*\\asymp n^{-2/5}\\). Combining its upper bound with (41),\n\\[\n \\frac{r_{\\mathrm{test}}(n)}{\\rho_n^*}\n \\ge c\\Lambda_n^{1/4}n^{\\,2/5-1/4}\n =c\\Lambda_n^{1/4}n^{3/20}\\longrightarrow\\infty.                    \\tag{42}\n\\]\nThus \\(r_{\\mathrm{test}}\\) is not of the order of the critical radius even in a legal regime already solved on the identical class. Whether or not \\(r_{\\mathrm{mix}}\\) happens to match this loose deterministic quantity, the requested conclusion \\(\\rho_n^*\\asymp r_{\\mathrm{sharp}}\\) cannot follow from the frozen pair. This proves the negative resolution.",
